.TH AFFIX 8 "2026-06-06" "affix 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
affix \- CPU placement policy daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B affix
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B affix
is a CPU placement policy daemon.  On Linux it watches process creation events,
listens for power-profile changes, and reconciles matching processes against
the active policy in
.BR affix.toml (5).
.PP
The Linux daemon is intended to run as a system service when cpuset enforcement
is required.  The installed systemd system unit runs
.B /usr/local/bin/affix
as root by default, because creating cpuset cgroups, migrating processes, and
adjusting systemd units require privileges on the cgroup hierarchy and D-Bus.
.SH RUNTIME POLICY MODEL
Affix reconciles only processes that match configured rule families.  A process
with no matching rule family is ignored.  Affix does not apply
operating-system-default policy to every process on the system.
.PP
If a matching rule family resolves to no effective affinity under the active
power profile, affinity for that family is operating-system default.  Untouched
processes are left alone.  Processes previously moved by affix into an
affix-owned cgroup are moved back to their recorded origin cgroup when possible.
Systemd units changed by affix are restored to the recorded original runtime
.B AllowedCPUs=
value.
.PP
The daemon does not preserve arbitrary historical process affinity or priority
state.  Its model is to apply the policy that is effective now, or to return to
operating-system default and the surrounding administrative envelope when no
affix policy applies.
.SH SYSTEMD TARGETING
For concrete affinity rules, affix first uses systemd runtime
.B AllowedCPUs=
when the process maps to a specific systemd unit.  System services are adjusted
through the system manager.  Service unit names do not need to match executable
names; the containing service is already the ownership boundary.  Desktop app
scopes under
.B user@UID.service
are adjusted through that user's systemd manager.
.PP
Affix connects to the user manager at
.B /run/user/UID/bus
when targeting a user app scope.  Systemd exposes
.B AllowedCPUs
on D-Bus as a byte-array CPU bitmap, not as the textual CPU list accepted by
.BR systemctl (1).
Affix converts its CPU mask and calls runtime
.B SetUnitProperties .
The change is not written to unit files.
.PP
For desktop app scopes and app slices, affix requires the unit name to match
the process identity.  For example, a KDE Konsole process in
.B app-org.kde.konsole-123.scope
may cause affix to update that scope.  An unrelated program launched inside
Konsole does not cause affix to update the whole Konsole scope.
.PP
Broad units such as
.BR session-*.scope ,
.BR user@UID.service ,
and generic slices are not direct targets for image rules.  Mutating those units
would affect processes that the rule did not identify.
.SH AFFIX-OWNED CGROUP FALLBACK
If a process is under a desktop app or background lineage but has no matching
app-specific systemd unit of its own, affix may fall back to moving the process
into an affix-owned cpuset cgroup.  This is the intended fallback for cases such
as an application binary launched from a terminal app scope where the child did
not receive its own app scope.
.PP
Affix-owned cgroups are allocated by normalized effective CPU mask.  Distinct
rules that resolve to the same effective mask share the same cgroup.  Leaf
cgroup names use the
.B affix-
prefix.
.PP
For affix-owned cpuset cgroups, affix intersects the requested CPU mask with the
process origin cgroup's
.B cpuset.cpus.effective
and the process scheduler affinity mask.  If the intersection is empty, the rule
cannot be enforced for that process.
.SH SYSTEMD CPU ENVELOPES
.B AllowedCPUs=
and
.B CPUAffinity=
are different systemd mechanisms.
.PP
.B AllowedCPUs=
is a cgroup v2 cpuset resource-control property.  Its effective value is limited
by parent units and is reported by systemd as
.BR EffectiveCPUs= .
.PP
.B CPUAffinity=
is scheduler affinity inherited by processes launched by systemd.  A cpuset that
allows CPUs
.B 0-31
does not make CPUs
.B 0-15
usable for a process that inherited scheduler affinity
.BR 16-31 .
The kernel schedules on the intersection of scheduler affinity, cpuset
constraints, and online CPUs.  Affix respects that intersection instead of
trying to undo administrative policy.
.SH BROAD SLICE POLICY
Some useful policies are slice policies rather than image policies.  For
example, putting all of
.B background.slice
on efficient cores is better expressed directly through systemd:
.PP
.nf
systemctl --user set-property --runtime background.slice AllowedCPUs=8-15
.fi
.PP
Persist the same idea with a user-manager drop-in if it should survive
restarts.  In that model affix has nothing to restore, because the administrator
owns the slice policy.  Do not approximate broad slice policy with broad affix
image selectors; that either affects no process because affix refuses broad
ownership, or it unnecessarily moves processes away from useful systemd
accounting when fallback ownership is permitted.
.SH PROCESS TREE RECONCILIATION
Affix resolves each process independently during subtree traversal.  A matched
parent supplies an inherited target to descendants, but each child is checked
against the rule set before that inherited target is used.  A child with its own
applicable rule and its descendants use the child target.
.PP
Power-profile changes trigger reconciliation of the live process tree.  Affix
does not poll for power mode in a loop; it reacts to the platform power-profile
notification layer.
.SH FAILURE AND RESTART BEHAVIOR
If a systemd D-Bus update fails for a specific unit, affix logs the error.  If
the process is eligible for affix-owned cgroup fallback, affix may still enforce
the affinity through that fallback.  Otherwise the process is skipped.
.PP
Origin cgroups for moved processes and original systemd
.B AllowedCPUs=
values are kept in daemon memory.  If affix restarts, those records are lost.
The daemon can still apply current policy, but it cannot restore placement or
runtime unit state that only the previous daemon process knew.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B affix
Run the daemon in the foreground.  Under systemd this is the command used by
both the system and user unit files.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/affix/affix.toml
Linux system service configuration.  This file is not created automatically by
the Linux daemon.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/affix/affix.toml
Linux user-mode configuration path.  If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, the fallback is
.B $HOME/.config/affix/affix.toml .
Relative or empty XDG paths are ignored.  If
.B HOME
is also unset, empty, or relative, affix falls back to the Linux system
configuration path.
.TP
.B /var/lib/affix/applications.sqlite
Linux system persistent decision database.
.TP
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/affix/applications.sqlite
Linux user-mode persistent decision database.  If
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
is unset, the fallback is
.B $HOME/.local/state/affix/applications.sqlite .
Relative or empty XDG paths are ignored.  If
.B HOME
is also unset, empty, or relative, affix falls back to the Linux system
database path.
.TP
.B /usr/local/share/affix/affix.example.toml
Linux example configuration installed by
.B just install .
Copy this file to
.B /etc/affix/affix.toml
to make it active for the system service.
.TP
.B /usr/local/lib/systemd/system/affix.service
System service unit installed by
.B just install .
.SH CONFIGURATION
See
.BR affix.toml (5).
.SH NOTES
The Linux install recipe does not enable or start the systemd service.  Enable
or start it explicitly with
.BR systemctl (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR affix.toml (5),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
